Game is a true, utter mess mix of up and downs with the end result being around 6-7/10 for me. Decent, ok, good enough but I expected more. Has to be said that I checked it recently, over 2 years after release and after hefty discount. If I were to review it then for the initial price it would probably still thumbs up but more like 5,5/10.

Also I was surprised I kinda liked the new mercenaries game I could not stand in previous incarnation. I wish though playing as shades wouldn't suck so much. Both humans are decent and kind of alike, playing as big mommy would always be fun and yet is done surprisingly well while swinging that slow ass hammer is a torture.

Little explanation what I mean by +ups and -downs:
+start of the game, admiring visuals and smoothness (RE engine still looks and runs good);
+initial village segment;
-infinite lycan spawn;
-lycans on horses and firing flaming arrow (is this for real?);
+castle segment and more open village;
+psycho doll segment;
-burping fish segment;
-more annoying lycan spawns and fire arrows;
+factory segment (though not as good as castle);
+mecha boss fight;
-return of annoying lycans yet again.

- No survival horror;
- No inventory management;
- No exploration; 
- Puzzles for babies;
- Plot is stupid;
- The game gives you inf ammo for a boss fight;
- You can't change FOV (without using a mod);
- Hard mode is not hard;
- The dropping system is the worst in the series (every single enemy drops something)

"herp derp big mommy milkers, me give thumbs up"

I've been a Resident Evil fan ever since the first game in 1996. I know what the franchise used to be and what it is now - and what it is now is... confusing.

Resident Evil Village is a very beautiful game with some cool mechanics (like the new inventory system), and some very memorable characters, but aside from that it's basically a game that doesn't know what path to take. CAPCOM wasted a very good opportunity to focus on the Dimitrescu castle, by spreading a good base story into many "bosses" that you have to defeat, each feeling like it's own little universe and a total different game. 

- You start on a village with zombies. OK;
- You go to a castle with a giant vampire lady. Meh, OK;
- This lady and her "mother" have experimented with a mold that turned 3 girls into flies, and these flies have a collective conscience that is basically that of a human. What?;
- The castle part was OK, but it was super short. You just killed this giant lady and will go onto a total different boss now, one that controls dolls;
- Super short as always, after you've killed the "doll lady" you'll basically go fight Megneto;
- After that, you'll fight the "final boss", Mother Miranda.

Playing this game will feel like playing Resident Evil, Silent Hill, Cyberpunk 2077, and Far Cry. And don't kid yourself, after finishing the game you won't feel as if the story makes any sense - apart from Ethan and his family, which is actually a nice continuation from Re7.

It's like 3 OK stories that don't connect put together with Re7.

Enemies are super slow and stupid, even the bosses. You'll yawn many times while fighting Lady D, the Doll Lady, Heisenberg, and even Mother Miranda. Not once will you feel actually in danger while fighting the bosses and most enemies - only in the beginning is where you actually feel like an outsider and that everyone's out to get you.
The daughters' are cute, but non-threatening. Boring.

CAPCOM should fire everyone responsible for writing the dialogue of this game and give their bonuses to the level and gameplay designers.

Fun and satisfying gameplay if you're looking for a more fine-tuned RE7 combat experience;
The story might leave some with an empty feeling reminiscent of a good old fashion retcon.

Probably one of my favorite story-horror games. 
Although it doesn't dive completely into the horror experience, it definitely has that suspense of a survival game. The graphics are absolutely beautiful, unique characters (and the character design), setting, everything is over the top amazing. Coming from someone who had played Resident Evil 7 previously- I personally prefer this second half of the story than the first. One of my favorite purchases I've made this past year, and probably one of the best games overall I've played in a while. Only issue I had was sometimes the cut scenes having delayed audio, or after alt tabbing the game lost fps. 

Otherwise, beautifully put game, and it was easy to put yourself into the hands of the protagonist. Brace yourself for tears ;-;

My 6 Reasons Against Buying This Game: OG RE FANS: This is more BioShock than resident evil, just look at the hands. I gave it a shot though, without expectations, and there are several reasons that I can not recommend this game. 1) I am someone who appreciates a challenge, yet try Village of Shadows difficulty from new game, (not new game +) and see how you fare with that. Your bullets won't affect your enemies unless you have upgraded your weapons already, making that difficulty level almost a moot point for those who aren't into the New Game + mode. (2) Most of the good unlockables are unlocked by simply upgrading your weapons fully, making it more of a (3) Guided Treasure Hunt (yes guided by red areas on the map) so that as long as you keep an eye on your map, you will likely find all treasure. The first person shooter does not make things more scary, (4) but seems as if Resident Evil is going to try to get into the competitive shooter market. I really wish the series would head back to its' roots. If that couldn't be achieved, then at least build off of the RE4 gameplay, which was far more interactive. In RE 4, when you shot a zombies arm (wtf is up with Lycans btw?) or knee cap, hip, etc., the zombie would react accordingly by swaying or falling into an area that could also affect other zombies. (5) With this one, there isn't any of that. Pop 6 rounds into a zombies chest and move on. The faster you shoot the better. (6) There is no account for accuracy, deaths, save files. There aren't any interactive scenes that can kill you, and well the story line is embarrassing, with sub par dialogue.

{{SPOILER}}
This will be my last Resident Evil purchase.

Get it on sale.
The game tries to please everyone, and attract new casual/console players. Here is what you should know before hand: 

- Big vampire lady's mechanics are awful. You can literally make her spin infinitely if you are on the opposite end of a table and outrun her at any time;
- You can make enemies disappear and respawn if you keep leaving their patrol area;  
- Heisenberg is a super edgy Hollywood character;
- The only scary part is the doll house, which also had the most interesting (yet rapidly dismissed in the story) character. Everything else is a matter of "oh there is the enemy sound cue, where is he showing up?";
- There is a trader (their RE4 reference), but is nothing as interesting as the RE4 merchant. He has a food cooking mechanic... completely irrelevant;
- After vampire lady's castle, you are just running and shooting most of the time;
- The game has Very nice graphics;
- The story is reeeaaaally really badly written, which may attract casual/console players, so this might be ok.

